PATCH 586: the MEMMGR resize DGROUP:0xd4 = 1917 -- the drivers' init and the compiled-sprite finalizer
give their surplus paragraphs back, as the original's blocks show.

board:0025 / board:0027.  DGROUP:0xd4 (0f69:2287, linear 0x11917) is `push ax ; call 1345 ; jb fail ;
mov cx,ds ; mov ds,ax ; pop ax ; push cx ; call 0cce ; pop ds ; retf`: find the block owned by CX:BX,
split its tail beyond AX paragraphs back onto the free list (0cce, patch 188).  Three callers, none
of which the port had threaded:

  MGAVIDEO init (driver asm 0x5e-0x74)   `mov ax,0x46a0 ; add ax,0xf ; rcr/shr x3 ; mov bx,0x3e4 ;
         mov cx,ds ; lcall [0xd4]` -- after copying its five data templates out (f98b), the driver
         keeps 0x46a of its 0x7ca paragraphs.  The port's init (patch 035) called it, but the linear
         was routed to a sound-driver stand-in that searched for the 0x4fa work object and resized
         nothing.  The oracle's MGAVIDEO block is 0x46a paragraphs; the port's was 0x7ca.
  SOUNDDVR init (driver asm 0xae-0xb9)   `mov ax,0x413 ; sub ax,0 ; mov bx,0x4fa ; mov cx,ds ;
         lcall [0xd4]` -- 0x413 of 0x434.  The port's call passed nothing.
  MGAVIDEO 21d6 (asm 0x22a1-0x22b6)      the compiled-sprite finalizer of the cockpit-instrument
         analyzer 2004: `mov ax,di ; add ax,0xf ; rcr/shr x3 ; lea bx,[bx] ; mov cx,ds ; lcall [0xd4]`
         -- each 0xc00-byte scratch (2004's alloc1) shrinks to the compacted sprite: the oracle's
         three M1 children are 0x20/0x15/0x18 paragraphs, the port's stayed 0xc0.  Arg-less before.

  The heap the original has at the cockpit switch is the heap the port now has: with 14 KB of driver
  image and 8 KB of scratch returned, the free tail at the switch is 32064 bytes, M2CON.MRL (26160)
  loads into it, and the Bradley console renders as the oracle's 60-second frame (86 % of the console
  rows pixel-identical; the rest is the AUTO CONTROL label, the radar and the dynamic instruments --
  board:0027).  Before, the load ran the MEMMGR's out-of-memory path (board:0025) and the block was
  handed to the next allocation.

  The shim's fist_icall no longer routes 0x11917 to the stand-in (re_out/fist_icall.c; the stand-in and
  its never-read g_snd_workobj_seg are gone from re_out/fist_sb.c).

